36\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"R16 | 205k | Resistor_SMD:R_0603_1608Metric | Device:R | Resistor | LCSC=C5126104, MPN=FRC0603F2053TS\"}, {\"source\": \"spice\", \"quote\": \"R17 FB +12V 1.8Meg\"}, {\"source\": \"spice\", \"quote\": \"V_12V +12V 0 DC 12.0\"}], \"impact\": \"The +12V boost rail would regulate near 5 V, underpowering anything fed from +12V and possibly mismatching the rail's intended loads.\", \"confidence\": 0.3}, {\"category\": \"other\", \"components\": [\"Q1\", \"U6\"], \"pins\": [\"Q1.2\", \"Q1.3\", \"U6.1\", \"U6.6\"], \"nets\": [\"+5V\", \"USBC_VBUS_IN\", \"Net-(Q1-G)\"], \"observed\": \"The LTC4412 ideal-diode PMOS Q1 has its source on +5V (Q1.2) and its drain on USBC_VBUS_IN (Q1.3). U6.1 (VIN) is on USBC_VBUS_IN and U6.6 (SENSE) is on +5V. The PMOS body diode therefore conducts from the +5V output back toward the USB input, which is the reverse-current path the ideal-diode arrangement is meant to block.\", \"expected\": \"For LTC4412 OR-ing, the PMOS source should be on the input supply (VIN side) and its drain on the load, with SENSE on the load side, so the body diode blocks reverse current.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"Q1 | Q_PMOS_GSD | Package_TO_SOT_SMD:SOT-23 | Device:Q_PMOS_GSD | P-MOSFET transistor, gate/source/drain\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"+5V [High Power] : ... Q1.2(S,passive)\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"USBC_VBUS_IN [High Power] : J3.A4(VBUS,passive) ... Q1.3(D,passive) R11.1(passive) R64.2(2,passive) U25.5(VBUS,passive) U6.1(VIN,power_in)\"}], \"impact\": \"Reverse current could leak from the 5 V rail back toward the USB source, and the input path may not behave as an ideal diode. This is consistent with the spec's note that the USB power path is unresolved.\", \"confidence\": 0.2}]}",
